1
00:00:00,258 --> 00:00:03,674
Rotablation for calcified
in-stent restenosis.

2
00:00:04,243 --> 00:00:08,446
By Dr. Mohd Maqbool Sohil,
Interventional Cardiologist.

3
00:00:08,891 --> 00:00:13,022
In-stent restenosis is re-narrowing
inside an earlier stent,

4
00:00:13,117 --> 00:00:15,962
as tissue called neointima
grows through it.

5
00:00:16,567 --> 00:00:22,865
After a drug-eluting stent, it affects up
to about 10% of patients within ten years.

6
00:00:23,671 --> 00:00:27,945
Common causes are an under-expanded
stent held back by calcium,

7
00:00:28,016 --> 00:00:33,028
calcified neointima, and new fatty
plaque, called neo-atherosclerosis.

8
00:00:33,751 --> 00:00:38,001
Symptoms are usually chest pain
or breathlessness on exertion.

9
00:00:38,606 --> 00:00:43,356
About one in five patients present
with an acute coronary syndrome.

10
00:00:43,991 --> 00:00:48,643
Angiography shows the pattern,
graded by Mehran: focal, diffuse,

11
00:00:48,693 --> 00:00:52,455
proliferative beyond the stent
edges, or total occlusion.

12
00:00:53,991 --> 00:00:58,620
IVUS or OCT shows the cause:
struts sitting on thick calcium,

13
00:00:58,670 --> 00:01:00,908
and a badly under-expanded stent.

14
00:01:01,524 --> 00:01:10,917
A calcium arc > 180°, thickness > 0.5 mm,
and length > 5 mm predict poor expansion.

15
00:01:11,591 --> 00:01:13,996
Even high-pressure balloons may fail.

16
00:01:14,541 --> 00:01:19,279
The balloon opens at the ends but not
in the middle: the dog-bone sign.

17
00:01:19,991 --> 00:01:23,881
Options include high-pressure,
scoring or cutting balloons,

18
00:01:23,931 --> 00:01:27,265
intravascular lithotripsy,
and excimer laser.

19
00:01:27,858 --> 00:01:32,715
Rotablation is used when devices
cannot cross or expand the lesion.

20
00:01:33,391 --> 00:01:38,817
Ablating stent metal risks a stuck burr,
so experts advise extreme caution,

21
00:01:38,867 --> 00:01:41,950
experienced operators,
and surgical back-up.

22
00:01:42,571 --> 00:01:43,511
Step one.

23
00:01:44,164 --> 00:01:46,104
A guide catheter is engaged,

24
00:01:46,223 --> 00:01:52,021
and a dedicated 0.009-inch RotaWire
is passed through the old stent.

25
00:01:52,771 --> 00:02:02,364
The burr, usually 1.25–1.5 mm,
spins at 135,000–180,000 rpm,

26
00:02:02,414 --> 00:02:06,723
in short runs of up to 30
s, with a continuous flush.

27
00:02:07,316 --> 00:02:11,292
Temporary pacing may be considered
for the right coronary artery.

28
00:02:11,971 --> 00:02:12,780
Step two.

29
00:02:13,409 --> 00:02:15,621
The diamond-coated burr pecks forward,

30
00:02:15,671 --> 00:02:20,659
sanding away calcified tissue and,
where the stent is crushed, its struts.

31
00:02:21,230 --> 00:02:27,111
Ninety-eight percent of particles are under
10 µm, smaller than a red blood cell.

32
00:02:28,971 --> 00:02:29,911
Step three.

33
00:02:30,564 --> 00:02:33,492
A non-compliant balloon now expands fully,

34
00:02:33,659 --> 00:02:36,635
cracking the calcium and
opening the old stent.

35
00:02:37,971 --> 00:02:38,876
Step four.

36
00:02:39,576 --> 00:02:44,230
A new drug-eluting stent is deployed
inside the old one, edge to edge.

37
00:02:44,885 --> 00:02:46,528
This is stent-in-stent.

38
00:02:47,971 --> 00:02:48,923
Step five.

39
00:02:49,480 --> 00:02:54,123
High-pressure post-dilatation presses
both stent layers against the wall.

40
00:02:54,971 --> 00:02:59,840
Final IVUS or OCT aims
for > 80% expansion,

41
00:03:00,030 --> 00:03:04,233
and the final angiogram shows a
smooth lumen with normal flow.

42
00:03:05,391 --> 00:03:08,412
European guidelines recommend
a drug-eluting stent

43
00:03:08,462 --> 00:03:11,724
or a drug-coated balloon
for in-stent restenosis.

44
00:03:12,293 --> 00:03:18,567
The 2024 guideline favours a new drug-eluting
stent inside a drug-eluting stent.

45
00:03:19,222 --> 00:03:23,281
A drug-coated balloon is an
alternative in selected patients.

46
00:03:24,011 --> 00:03:30,928
In small series, rotablation of
undilatable stents succeeded in 85–100%,

47
00:03:31,023 --> 00:03:35,666
with repeat procedures
in 13–41% at one year.

48
00:03:36,259 --> 00:03:39,101
The ARTIST trial showed
routine rotablation for

49
00:03:39,151 --> 00:03:42,866
restenosis is not better
than balloon angioplasty.

50
00:03:43,591 --> 00:03:48,874
Afterwards, dual antiplatelet therapy
usually continues for about six months,

51
00:03:48,924 --> 00:03:50,258
then one agent.

52
00:03:50,815 --> 00:03:53,017
Never stop it without advice.

53
00:03:53,612 --> 00:03:59,553
Control risk factors, join cardiac
rehabilitation, and report chest pain promptly.

54
00:04:00,251 --> 00:04:01,156
Key points.

55
00:04:01,760 --> 00:04:06,877
Rotablation is a specialised option
for undilatable, calcified restenosis,

56
00:04:06,927 --> 00:04:11,618
guided by imaging, and followed by
a new stent inside the old one.

57
00:04:12,271 --> 00:04:14,676
The sources are shown on screen.

58
00:04:19,751 --> 00:04:23,144
For education only, not medical advice.

59
00:04:23,737 --> 00:04:27,475
Dr. Mohd Maqbool Sohil,
Interventional Cardiologist.

